WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.483
Elena, a dental hygienist, explains the categories to Theo.

00:00:04.483 --> 00:00:09.963
Diagnostic and preventive services are exams, x-rays, cleanings, fluoride and sealants.

00:00:09.963 --> 00:00:13.450
Restorative services repair teeth: fillings, crowns, inlays.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:18.431
Oral surgery covers extractions and other surgery of the mouth.

00:00:18.431 --> 00:00:22.416
Endodontics treats the tooth’s interior, chiefly root canals.

00:00:22.416 --> 00:00:25.405
Periodontics treats the gums and bone.

00:00:25.405 --> 00:00:29.888
If Theo loses a tooth, a specialist can replace

00:00:29.888 --> 00:00:34.371
it with a bridge, a denture or an implant.

00:00:34.371 --> 00:00:36.862
That specialty is called prosthodontics.

00:00:36.862 --> 00:00:40.847
Orthodontics straightens teeth with braces, often limited to

00:00:40.847 --> 00:00:44.334
children and carrying its own lifetime maximum.

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:50.810
Plans group these categories into tiers that determine how much the plan pays.

00:00:50.810 --> 00:00:55.180
An indemnity dental plan lets the patient choose any dentist.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.986
A scheduled plan pays a fixed dollar amount per procedure, such

00:00:59.986 --> 00:01:04.793
as 90 dollars for a filling, and the patient pays the

00:01:04.793 --> 00:01:09.600
rest; schedules are easy to understand but lag behind rising fees.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.717
A nonscheduled plan pays a percentage of usual, customary and reasonable charges by benefit

00:01:15.717 --> 00:01:21.835
category: typically 100 percent of preventive, 80 percent of basic services such as fillings

00:01:21.835 --> 00:01:27.516
and extractions, and 50 percent of major services such as crowns and bridges.

00:01:27.516 --> 00:01:33.196
A combination plan uses a schedule for some services and percentages for others.

00:01:33.196 --> 00:01:38.440
Dental HMO and PPO networks apply the same categories with network pricing.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:43.920
Most dental plans carry a small annual deductible, often 50 dollars,

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.400
waived for preventive care, and an annual maximum benefit, commonly 1,000

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:53.883
to 2,000 dollars per person, which resets each year.

00:01:53.883 --> 00:01:59.363
Marcus needs a crown with a UCR charge of 1,200 dollars

00:01:59.363 --> 00:02:04.843
under a 100 80 50 plan with a 50 dollar deductible.

00:02:04.843 --> 00:02:10.821
He pays the deductible, then 50 percent of the remaining 1,150, which

00:02:10.821 --> 00:02:16.301
is 575, for a total of 625; the plan pays 575.

00:02:16.301 --> 00:02:22.777
If the plan has a 1,500 dollar annual maximum and he already used

00:02:22.777 --> 00:02:29.253
1,200 this year, the plan pays only 300 and he pays the rest.

00:02:29.253 --> 00:02:32.740
Maximums, not percentages, often decide the bill.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:38.392
Dental plans exclude cosmetic work such as whitening, replacement of lost or stolen dentures,

00:02:38.392 --> 00:02:43.640
experimental procedures and often teeth missing before coverage began, the missing tooth clause.

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:48.081
Limitations include frequency limits, two cleanings a year, waiting periods of

00:02:48.081 --> 00:02:52.521
six to twelve months for major services, and the least expensive

00:02:52.521 --> 00:02:56.962
alternative treatment rule, which pays for the cheapest professionally acceptable fix.

00:02:56.962 --> 00:03:02.614
For costly work above a threshold, often 300 dollars, plans ask for predetermination of

00:03:02.614 --> 00:03:08.266
benefits: the dentist submits the treatment plan, the insurer states what it will pay

00:03:08.266 --> 00:03:13.514
before work begins, and the patient can decide with the numbers in hand.

00:03:13.514 --> 00:03:16.340
Predetermination is an estimate, not a guarantee.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:19.276
Priya may add dental as part of the

00:03:19.276 --> 00:03:21.845
medical plan or as a separate plan.

00:03:21.845 --> 00:03:26.249
An integrated deductible counts dental and medical expenses toward one combined deductible,

00:03:26.249 --> 00:03:30.653
which can leave routine dental unpaid until the larger deductible is met.

00:03:30.653 --> 00:03:35.792
A stand-alone dental plan has its own small deductible and annual maximum and pays

00:03:35.792 --> 00:03:40.563
preventive care from the first visit, which is why most employers choose it.

00:03:40.563 --> 00:03:44.600
Pediatric dental is an essential health benefit, so children’s dental must

00:03:44.600 --> 00:03:48.637
be available with individual and small group medical coverage, either embedded

00:03:48.637 --> 00:03:51.940
or through a stand-alone dental plan on the Exchange.

00:03:51.940 --> 00:03:55.678
Dental care is predictable, so people who need expensive work

00:03:55.678 --> 00:03:59.415
are the first to sign up for voluntary dental coverage.

00:03:59.415 --> 00:04:03.153
Plans defend themselves with the tools you have seen before.

00:04:03.153 --> 00:04:07.264
Waiting periods of six to twelve months for basic and major

00:04:07.264 --> 00:04:11.001
services stop someone from enrolling for a crown and leaving.

00:04:11.001 --> 00:04:13.991
Participation requirements keep healthy mouths in the pool.

00:04:13.991 --> 00:04:18.476
Late enrollees may be limited to preventive benefits for the first year.

00:04:18.476 --> 00:04:22.961
Annual maximums cap the plan’s exposure, orthodontics carries a lifetime maximum, and

00:04:22.961 --> 00:04:27.073
the missing tooth clause excludes work on teeth lost before coverage.

00:04:27.073 --> 00:04:30.810
Each tool trades a little coverage for an affordable premium.
